---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Leave Pay by State 2026: Who Publishes These Estimates';
const description = 'Leave Pay by State, 2026: who publishes these unemployment, disability and paid leave estimates, what they rest on, and what the site never does for you.';
const n = K.PROGRAMS.filter((p) => p.status === 'paying').length;
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Leave Pay by State</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>Someone who has just been laid off, or who is about to give birth, asks a simple question: how much will come in each week, and for how long. The official answer is split between offices. The unemployment calculator of one state knows nothing of its disability program, the paid leave calculator of another asks for eight weeks of pay stubs and says nothing about taxes, and the search results in between are law firm pages and copied tables from earlier years.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public rules. It is not a state agency, a law firm, an insurer or a payroll company. It files no claim, sells no coverage, has no partner to send you to and carries no affiliate link.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What it relies on</h2>
    <p>The U.S. Department of Labor table of state unemployment laws in force on July 1, 2026, for {K.STATES.length} jurisdictions; the pages and statutes of the {n} disability and paid leave programs paying in 2026, from California's Employment Development Department to the District of Columbia's Office of Paid Family Leave; the 2026 federal tax brackets of Revenue Procedure 2025-32 and the IRS rulings on benefit taxation.</p>
    <h2>What it leaves out</h2>
    <p>Whether you qualify, the reason you left a job, your employer's own short-term disability plan and any judgment on your claim. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every simplification; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for a cap that changed or a formula we read wrong. We answer questions about a figure or a document, not about your own claim, which only the state agency can decide.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
